to that generic chocolate. Or are cakla and nuncitka intrinsically<BR>
different types of things?<BR>
&gt;&gt; <BR>
How so, generic chocolate is presumably abstract as {le cakla} distinctly is not.&nbsp; But {le nu mi citka lo cakla} is abstract already.&nbsp; Yes, citka and nuncitka are inherently different things, one concrete, one abstract.&nbsp; One might not exist, one always does. And so on.<BR>
<BR>
&lt;&lt;<BR>
&gt;when I am reporting on someone else {lo nu} seems safer, since his<BR>
&gt;particulars may not be obvious (if we get it by observing, we may miss<BR>
&gt;details that are significant to him;<BR>
<BR>
It would certainly be a safer claim, but it would not be equivalent<BR>
to what we mean in English by "he likes to eat chocolate".<BR>
&gt;&gt;<BR>
So you keep saying, but when asked why,&nbsp; you give an argument about quantifiers, whose relevance is at least obscure, or one about occasions, which is demonstrably irrelevant.&nbsp; Do you know something about liking to eat chocolate that I don't?&nbsp; What?<BR>
